   "summary": "ASEAN leaders have opened the possibility for Myanmar's political representatives to gradually return to high-level meetings, contingent on Myanmar's progress in implementing the Five-Point Consensus. The approach will be phased and progress-based, with ASEAN expressing deep concern over the insufficient progress made by Myanmar so far.",
   "summary_zh": "东盟领导人已为缅甸政治代表逐步重返高级别会议打开可能，前提是缅甸在落实《五点共识》方面取得进展。相关安排将分阶段推进，并以进展为依据；东盟对缅甸迄今进展不足表示深切关切。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Already have Rappler+? Sign in to listen to groundbreaking journalism. RETREAT. Leaders and representatives gather for the 49th ASEAN Summit retreat session at the Coconut Palace in Pasay City on November 15, 2026. ASEAN leaders have opened the possibility for Myanmar's political representatives to gradually return to high-level meetings, contingent on Myanmar's progress in implementing the Five-Point Consensus. The approach will be phased and progress-based, with ASEAN expressing deep concern over the insufficient progress made by Myanmar so far. This is AI-generated. Read the article for full context. Report any errors . MANILA, Philippines – Leaders of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) have opened a path to a possible “step-by-step” return of Myanmar’s political representatives to high-level meetings, although this remains conditional on Myanmar demonstrating concrete progress in implementing the bloc’s peace plan. “Subject to sustained and concrete progress in the implementation of the 5PC by Myanmar, ASEAN may consider the gradual restoration of Myanmar’s full political representation,” said the document adopted on Sunday, November 15, the first day of the 49th ASEAN Summit and Related Summits in Manila. The regional bloc has previously left the door open for Myanmar’s political return. But the decision makes the possibility of a gradual restoration more explicit, even as leaders maintained existing restrictions. In 2021, Myanmar’s military launched a coup that ousted the elected government of Aung San Suu Kyi and plunged the country into a bloody civil war. Since then, ASEAN has barred Myanmar’s political leaders from attending ASEAN summits and foreign ministers’ meetings, allowing only non-political representatives to participate. The decision comes amid calls from some member countries, particularly Thailand, to re-engage Myanmar’s military-backed government following elections held under military rule . The elections remain contentious, however, with ASEAN leaders only saying they “noted the conclusion of the three phases of Myanmar’s general elections,” without explicitly endorsing its validity. Philippine Foreign Secretary Maria Theresa Lazaro, who also serves as ASEAN’s special envoy on Myanmar, explained that the step-by-step approach would depend on whether Myanmar follows the peace plan. “The step-by-step approach is adherence to the Five-Point Consensus. So, for example, the first one is cessation of hostilities,” Lazaro said in a press briefing on Sunday evening. “We want to see how Myanmar responds to that because right now, there’s still certain bombings and displacement of Myanmar people. So step-by-step, we give it to them, how they’re going to tackle this issue, and we monitor that,” she added. – Rappler.com Upgrade to Rappler+ for exclusive content and unlimited access",
